{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1718 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "6bec431594cf2ce5ebb707e7783b5ba5bb608dcdbea51cbdfb0b5c0ac792f757" :edb/db-before-t 1717 :edb/db-after-t 1718 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535030 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:13:53.574Z" 13194139535030 true] [17592186083147 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327783 13194139535030 true] [17592186083221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327786 13194139535030 true] [17592186083221 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327793 13194139535030 true] [17592186327783 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327784 13194139535030 true] [17592186327783 1089 "q-323218" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327783 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535030 true] [17592186327783 1100 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-7$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139535030 true] [17592186327783 1113 #uuid "3f82cc12-d956-5296-9d23-6bdadb185052" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327783 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 7.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327784 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327785 13194139535030 true] [17592186327784 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535030 true] [17592186327784 1041 "field-1" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327784 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327785 13194139535030 true] [17592186327784 1110 #uuid "3f0f861b-9518-5001-9a35-06d48a9f94df" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327785 1034 "6" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327785 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535030 true] [17592186327785 1111 #uuid "3db7807c-da93-5f6f-8bf5-832fec41bb95" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327786 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327787 13194139535030 true] [17592186327786 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327789 13194139535030 true] [17592186327786 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327791 13194139535030 true] [17592186327786 1089 "q-323819" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327786 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535030 true] [17592186327786 1100 "The polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-9x-21$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-3,-2).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." 13194139535030 true] [17592186327786 1113 #uuid "9930c420-f372-5a27-8b7c-8f38c6bfb53a" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327786 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-21$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-21}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 21,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-3,-2)$ is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{7}{3}$ | $3$ | $7$ | $-9$ | $-21$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-7$ | $0$ | $21$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{7}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{7}{3})(3{x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{7}{3})(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-\\frac{7}{3},$ $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327787 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327788 13194139535030 true] [17592186327787 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535030 true] [17592186327787 1041 "field-1" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327787 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327788 13194139535030 true] [17592186327787 1110 #uuid "d03e1aea-f397-5196-9173-82319cdcecfd" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327788 1034 "-\\frac{7}{3}" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327788 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535030 true] [17592186327788 1111 #uuid "d653916d-5138-5616-a1d4-14509ffa1ea1" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327789 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327790 13194139535030 true] [17592186327789 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535030 true] [17592186327789 1041 "field-2" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327789 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327790 13194139535030 true] [17592186327789 1110 #uuid "6b61613f-414a-5f86-b6d2-5d1181143f46" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327790 1034 "-\\sqrt{3}" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327790 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535030 true] [17592186327790 1111 #uuid "7d4146ea-ef74-54fb-b8a7-8f7978b7e5e7" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327792 13194139535030 true] [17592186327791 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535030 true] [17592186327791 1041 "field-3" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327791 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327792 13194139535030 true] [17592186327791 1110 #uuid "0bd080ed-486a-56f7-a976-1b1e5c9c2610" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327792 1034 "\\sqrt{3}" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327792 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535030 true] [17592186327792 1111 #uuid "09ce3642-5223-5ab2-bc23-63df075b970e" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327793 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327794 13194139535030 true] [17592186327793 1089 "q-38293" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327793 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535030 true] [17592186327793 1100 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6$ has one of its roots in the interval $(0,3),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327793 1113 #uuid "95a80834-eafd-54c7-aa21-4bf9e18db155" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327793 1145 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 6\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,3),$ are $1$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-1)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}-x-6).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-1)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+2)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=1,-2,3.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n1-2+3=2.\n$$" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327795 13194139535030 true] [17592186327794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327796 13194139535030 true] [17592186327794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327797 13194139535030 true] [17592186327794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327798 13194139535030 true] [17592186327794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327799 13194139535030 true] [17592186327794 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535030 true] [17592186327794 1041 "selection" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327794 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327795 13194139535030 true] [17592186327794 1110 #uuid "f7f7a183-39dd-5980-9f62-bb7338d90592" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327795 1034 "2" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327795 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535030 true] [17592186327795 1111 #uuid "d07b4d3a-0a17-5f2c-a16c-b0b451b8dc12" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327796 1034 "6" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327796 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535030 true] [17592186327796 1111 #uuid "43ce1fdf-8e8a-54cb-b634-270a6857697a" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327797 1034 "-3" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327797 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535030 true] [17592186327797 1111 #uuid "888c2c61-5acb-5360-8c19-a73b3edefb26" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327798 1034 "4" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327798 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535030 true] [17592186327798 1111 #uuid "70087bf9-e860-570f-9dde-409a5651d33a" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327799 1034 "-2" 13194139535030 true] [17592186327799 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535030 true] [17592